se two propositions that  freedom is something that really is  uniquely embraced by the West and that  that also is connected to the history of  slavery those things seem to me  something that could be usefully thought  about in the context of 19th century  Japan which is what most of my own work  focuses on and the reason for that is  that first of all 19th century Japan was  a place where freedom really is embraced  and becomes a kind of central part of  political discourse in especially in the  decades following the major restoration  of 1868 calm so that's the first thing  and then the second thing is that Japan  at that time is also a society where  slavery as an institution really does  not exist at earlier points in the past  there had been something like slavery  but most scholars agree that by the 18th  century or so slavery is pretty much  disappeared from Japanese society so  here we have a non-western society that  embraces freedom and doesn't have  slavery so I was interested in working  out you know what the case of Japan  might have to say then about these  bigger issues and what I conclude in a  kind of general way is that the idea of  slavery does become very important in  Japan in that period is a kind of  metaphor for talking about lots of  different groups in society who needed  to be set free so once these groups are  identified as slave like then you can  also start introducing this idea that  they need to be emancipated and that in  turn i think paves the way for a kind of  broader adoption of the rhetoric of 19th  century liberalism in japanese political  life ok and how did you come to write  the paper well as you mentioned I wrote  a book that took a long time about the  history of punishment ok which had lots  of lovely  you know things in it you know various  gruesome torches and horrible ways of  executing people and then also the kind  of exploitation of prison labor and so  on so pretty grim stuff so part of me  wanted to do something 